\label{lem:module}
  Let $G$ be a graph and $M,N$ be modules of $G$. Then
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item $M\cap N$ is a module of $G$;
  \item if $M\cap N\neq\varnothing$, then $M\cup N$ is a module of $G$; and
  \item if $N\not\subseteq M$ then $M\setminus N$ is a module of $G$.
  \end{enumerate}

\begin{equation*}
		V^\ast=\biguplus_{1\le i\le n}\vertices[H_i]
		\quad\quad\mbox{and}\quad\quad
		E^\ast=\left(\biguplus_{1\le i\le n}\gEdges[H_i]\right)\uplus\left\{xy\mid x\in \vertices[H_i],y\in\vertices[H_j],v_iv_j\in\gEdges[G]\right\}
\end{equation*}

This concept allows us to decompose graphs into prime graphs (via
Lemma~\ref{lem:newhope} below) and recover a tree structure for an
arbitrary graph.\footnote{This also allows us to consider prime graphs as generalised {non-decomposable}
  $n$-ary connectives. We will come back to this point in Section~\ref{sec:generalised}.}
  The two operations $\vlbin{\parr}$ and $\vlbin{\otimes}$, defined
in Definition~\ref{def:par} are then represented by the following two prime
graphs:
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:par-tens}
  \vlbin{\parr}\colon\quad \vbul1\qquad\vbul2
  \qquad\mbox{and}\qquad
  \vlbin{\otimes}\colon\quad \vbul3\qquad\vbul4
  \edges{n3/n4}
  \hskip3em
\end{equation}
We have $\vlbin{\parr}\connn{G,H}=G\vlbin{\parr} H$ and $\vlbin{\otimes}\connn{G,H}=G\vlbin{\otimes}
H$.

\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:par-tens}
  \vlbin{\parr}\colon\quad \vbul1\qquad\vbul2
  \qquad\mbox{and}\qquad
  \vlbin{\otimes}\colon\quad \vbul3\qquad\vbul4
  \edges{n3/n4}
  \hskip3em
\end{equation}

