	\;,
\end{equation*}
where $\mathcal{D}_n$ is the derivation for $C_n$ and $\mathcal{D}'$ exists by
induction hypothesis.

\begin{equation*}
		\small
		\ods{\varnothing}{\mathcal{D}_1}{C_1\Coons{\ods{\varnothing}{\mathcal{D}'}{C_{2}\coons{ \ldots C_n\coonso{R_n}}{R_{2}}}{}}{R_1}}{}
		\;,
	\end{equation*}



	We prove the two cases simultaneously, by induction on the size of the graph $G\vlbin{\parr} P\connn{M_1, \dots, M_n}$, as defined in  Definition~\ref{def:size}. Let $\mathcal{D}$ be the given derivation of $G\vlbin{\parr} P\connn{M_1, \dots, M_n}$.


	We aim to construct 
	$C\coonso{R}$, $\mathcal{D}_G$, $\mathcal{D}_C$, 
	and 
	either $K_i$, $\mathcal{D}_i$  for all $i\in \set{1,\dots, n}$, 
	or $K_X$, $K_Y$, $\mathcal{D}_X$, $\mathcal{D}_Y$, 
	satisfying the condition of the statement.

	We make a case analysis on the bottommost rule instance 
	in the derivation $\mathcal{D}$.

	We now proceed, systematically exhausting the cases (a) to (e) described in Section~\ref{sec:splitting}.
	To avoid redundancy in the proof,  we assume that whenever we define the context of the shape
	$C={C_n\coons{\ldots C_2\coons{C_1\coons{\cdot}{R_1}}{R_2}\ldots}{R_n}}$ 
